# What "Trim Titles" removes from album and track names.
#
# One pattern per line, and '*' matches anything.  A pattern is tested against
# the text inside a bracket group at the end of the name -- (...), [...] or
# {...} -- and against whatever follows a final " - ".  Matching ignores case.
#
#   feat. *     drops "(feat. Clairo)"
#   * Version   drops "(Taylor's Version)"
#   Live        drops "[Live]" and nothing longer
#   * Remaster  drops "- 2021 Remaster"
#
# A group at the start of a name is never touched, so "(I Can't Get No)
# Satisfaction" keeps its opening.  Delete a line to keep that wording; add one
# for anything this list misses.  Lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# Trimming works backwards from the end and stops at the first group it does
# not recognise.  So when a name will not trim, it is the LAST group that needs
# a pattern: "Slut! (Taylor's Version) (From The Vault)" needs one for "From
# The Vault" before the "Taylor's Version" in front of it can go.
#
# The tags on disk are not changed, and neither is the database.  Switch the
# setting off and every name comes back.  Switching it on again re-reads this
# file, so an edit takes effect without restarting the player.

# --- guest artists ---
feat *
feat. *
ft *
ft. *
featuring *
with *
w/ *

# --- alternate recordings ---
Live
Live *
Acoustic
Instrumental
Demo
Alternate *
Take *
* Take
Vocal *
Session
* Session

# --- remasters and reissues ---
Remaster
Remastered
Remaster *
Remastered *
Digitally *
* Remaster
* Remastered
* Master

# --- editions and versions ---
Version *
* Version
*ver.
From The Vault
Deluxe
Deluxe *
* Edition
* Release
Expanded *
Reissue *
Bonus *
* Bonus Track

# --- mixes and edits ---
Mix
Remix
Edit
Radio *
* Mix
* Mixes
* Remix
* Edit

# --- delivery labels ---
Explicit
Clean
Mono
Stereo
* Mono
* Stereo
Karaoke
Karaoke *
Original
Single
